WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.750
(dramatic music)

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:06.870
- Donald Trump gets a special prosecutor.

00:00:06.870 --> 00:00:09.450
It's the biggest, most
beautiful specialist counsel

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:11.250
anyone has ever seen.

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:13.500
No one's ever seen a
prosecutor quite so special.

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:17.073
Grown men were weeping at
the sight of this counsel.

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:19.470
That joke never gets old.

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:21.480
In a Friday after the midterms news dump,

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:23.040
Attorney General Merrick Garland announced

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:24.810
that he has appointed a special counsel

00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:26.970
to conduct two criminal investigations

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:28.380
related to Donald Trump.

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:29.790
This is extremely big news

00:00:29.790 --> 00:00:32.580
that has so far kind of
flown under the radar,

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:35.310
but in all likelihood
this means big things.

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:36.630
In a super abundance of caution,

00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:37.830
the attorney general has decided

00:00:37.830 --> 00:00:39.840
that the Department of
Justice can't investigate

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:40.830
the former president

00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:42.480
because he's running for president again

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:43.960
and it would look political.

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:45.440
- And isn't this sort
of like double jeopardy?

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:48.960
In the old days they used
to call it double jeopardy.

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:50.400
- Enter the special counsel.

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:51.690
No, not that guy.

00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:52.710
- You're not that guy, pal.

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:54.390
Trust me, you're not that guy.

00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:55.950
- This guy, Jack Smith.

00:00:55.950 --> 00:00:57.600
Should Donald Trump celebrate?

00:00:57.600 --> 00:00:59.670
Does this mean that there
will be a few more years

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:00.780
of Trump escaping justice,

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:03.450
or that prosecutions are
just around the horizon?

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:04.283
Well, by all counts,

00:01:04.283 --> 00:01:06.450
Attorney General Merrick
Garland is an institutionalist.

00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:09.120
Those that want to see a
former president put in jail

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:11.130
have criticized Garland for being too slow

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:13.680
and too methodical, or
just not doing anything.

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:15.060
And although some Republicans predicted

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:16.350
that Garland would use his office

00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:18.540
to exact some kind of
revenge on Republicans,

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:20.040
the opposite has largely been true.

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:21.270
It appears that Garland decided

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:23.970
not to prosecute Matt
Gaetz or Rudy Giuliani.

00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:25.860
And now Garland has issued an order

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:28.530
to appoint John L. Smith
to serve as special counsel

00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:29.730
for the Department of Justice

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:31.410
to conduct two ongoing investigations

00:01:31.410 --> 00:01:33.300
related to the former President Trump:

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:34.860
whether Trump unlawfully interfered

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:36.570
with the 2020 presidential election

00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:39.000
and the certification of
the Electoral College vote

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:40.230
on January 6th,

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:42.030
and whether Trump broke any laws

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:43.560
when he removed classified documents

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:44.730
and other presidential records

00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:46.710
from the White House to Mar-a-Lago.

00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:47.940
Now, Congress has delegated

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:49.230
the authority of the attorney general

00:01:49.230 --> 00:01:51.180
to appoint a special counsel.

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:53.617
28 U.S.C. 510 says, "The
attorney general may

00:01:53.617 --> 00:01:55.417
"from time to time make such provisions

00:01:55.417 --> 00:01:57.907
"as he considers appropriate
authorizing the performance

00:01:57.907 --> 00:02:00.127
"by any other officer, employee, or agency

00:02:00.127 --> 00:02:01.237
"of the Department of Justice

00:02:01.237 --> 00:02:03.390
"of any function of the attorney general."

00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:05.340
But the special counsel
does have real independence.

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:07.597
Under C.F.R. Section 600.7.,

00:02:07.597 --> 00:02:09.277
"The special counsel shall not be subject

00:02:09.277 --> 00:02:10.387
"to the day-to-day supervision

00:02:10.387 --> 00:02:12.007
"of any official of the department.

00:02:12.007 --> 00:02:13.867
"However, the attorney general may request

00:02:13.867 --> 00:02:15.967
"that the special counsel
provide an explanation

00:02:15.967 --> 00:02:18.277
"for any investigative
or prosecutorial step

00:02:18.277 --> 00:02:20.587
"and may after review
conclude that the action

00:02:20.587 --> 00:02:22.327
"was so inappropriate or unwarranted

00:02:22.327 --> 00:02:24.187
"under established departmental practices

00:02:24.187 --> 00:02:25.777
"that it should not be pursued.

00:02:25.777 --> 00:02:27.127
"In concluding that review,

00:02:27.127 --> 00:02:28.657
"the attorney general
will give great weight

00:02:28.657 --> 00:02:30.067
"to the views of the special counsel.

00:02:30.067 --> 00:02:32.257
"If the attorney general
concludes that a proposed action

00:02:32.257 --> 00:02:34.747
"by a special counsel
should not be pursued,

00:02:34.747 --> 00:02:37.500
"the attorney general
shall notify Congress."

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:38.730
And that section goes on to explain

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:41.310
that the special counsel can
only be removed for cause.

00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:42.900
Now, Garland's order is broader

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:44.190
with respect to the first issue,

00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:46.350
the certification over the
Electoral College vote.

00:02:46.350 --> 00:02:48.127
Quote, "The special counsel is authorized

00:02:48.127 --> 00:02:50.017
"to conduct the ongoing investigation

00:02:50.017 --> 00:02:52.537
"into whether any person
or entity violated the law

00:02:52.537 --> 00:02:54.247
"in connection with efforts to interfere

00:02:54.247 --> 00:02:56.137
"with the lawful transfer of power

00:02:56.137 --> 00:02:58.117
"following the 2020 presidential election

00:02:58.117 --> 00:03:00.277
"or the certification of of
the Electoral College vote

00:03:00.277 --> 00:03:02.707
"held on or about January 6, 2021,

00:03:02.707 --> 00:03:04.147
"as well as any matters that arose

00:03:04.147 --> 00:03:06.337
"or might arise directly
from this investigation

00:03:06.337 --> 00:03:10.860
"or that are within the scope
of 28 C.F.R. Section 600.4a."

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:12.907
And continues, "This
authorization does not apply

00:03:12.907 --> 00:03:14.287
"to prosecutions that
are currently pending

00:03:14.287 --> 00:03:15.277
"in the District of Columbia,

00:03:15.277 --> 00:03:17.827
"as well as future
investigations and prosecutions

00:03:17.827 --> 00:03:19.507
"of individuals for
offenses they committed

00:03:19.507 --> 00:03:21.877
"while physically present
on the Capitol grounds

00:03:21.877 --> 00:03:23.700
"on January 6, 2021."

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:25.680
That means that people who
were on the Capitol grounds

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:27.720
will continue to be prosecuted by the DOJ

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:29.880
with the involvement
of the special counsel.

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:30.900
That means that the normies

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:32.640
who are not in Trump's inner circle

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:33.810
who were on the Capitol grounds

00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:36.030
will continue to be prosecuted by the DOJ

00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:38.100
without the involvement
of the special counsel.

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:39.870
The special counsel will
be looking at the role

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:41.940
that Trump and others who
were not at the Capitol,

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:45.270
but who were scheming for ways
for him to stay in office.

00:03:45.270 --> 00:03:46.103
And as we've seen,

00:03:46.103 --> 00:03:47.400
this is a fairly complicated investigation

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:48.330
that encompasses the plot

00:03:48.330 --> 00:03:51.030
to use fake electors in multiple states.

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:53.190
It could also include
investigation of people

00:03:53.190 --> 00:03:55.230
who planned the January 6 protests,

00:03:55.230 --> 00:03:56.760
which could lead higher up the chain.

00:03:56.760 --> 00:03:58.740
And the special counsel
order does not impact

00:03:58.740 --> 00:04:00.480
ongoing state and local investigations,

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:02.250
such as the Fulton County's probe

00:04:02.250 --> 00:04:03.840
into the fake elector plot.

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:05.790
There's currently a grand
jury considering evidence

00:04:05.790 --> 00:04:06.720
in that case right now,

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:08.850
with possible defendants
including Donald Trump

00:04:08.850 --> 00:04:10.170
and Senator Lindsey Graham.

00:04:10.170 --> 00:04:11.700
And the special counsel
will also take over

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:14.190
the investigation into
the Mar-a-Lago documents.

00:04:14.190 --> 00:04:16.267
Quote, "The special counsel
is further authorized

00:04:16.267 --> 00:04:18.337
"to conduct the ongoing
investigation referenced

00:04:18.337 --> 00:04:20.137
"and described in the
United States' Response

00:04:20.137 --> 00:04:23.017
"to Motion for Judicial
Oversight and Additional Relief,

00:04:23.017 --> 00:04:25.087
"Donald J. Trump v. United States,

00:04:25.087 --> 00:04:26.587
"as well as any matters that arose

00:04:26.587 --> 00:04:28.687
"or may arise directly
from this investigation

00:04:28.687 --> 00:04:32.370
"or that are within the
scope of 28 C.F.R. 600.4a."

00:04:32.370 --> 00:04:35.190
So the question is, why a special counsel?

00:04:35.190 --> 00:04:37.470
The special or independent
counsel at the federal level

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:40.920
has been around in one form or
another for almost 150 years.

00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:42.540
The first independent
counsel was appointed

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:45.270
by President Ulysses S. Grant
to investigate corruption

00:04:45.270 --> 00:04:47.610
at the high level of
federal government officials

00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:48.480
in his administration.

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:50.880
And just how independent
these special prosecutors were

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:53.100
depended on the laws
governing their appointment.

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:54.570
After the fallout from Watergate,

00:04:54.570 --> 00:04:57.330
Congress passed the Ethics
in Government Act of 1978

00:04:57.330 --> 00:05:00.090
which authorized the appointment
of independent counsels.

00:05:00.090 --> 00:05:02.160
Under the act, the attorney
general would conduct

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:04.080
an initial investigation to determine

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:06.300
whether an independent
investigation was warranted.

00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:09.150
If it was, then a three-judge
panel of federal judges

00:05:09.150 --> 00:05:10.620
would appoint an independent counsel.

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:13.200
That act was used to investigate
Bill Clinton initially

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:14.910
for his Whitewater real estate deals,

00:05:14.910 --> 00:05:16.950
which eventually turned
into an investigation

00:05:16.950 --> 00:05:18.750
over his affair with Monica Lewinsky

00:05:18.750 --> 00:05:20.460
and obstruction of justice.

00:05:20.460 --> 00:05:22.320
In 1999, the statute expired

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:24.000
and Congress did not reauthorize it

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:26.040
in part because the
Democrats probably believed

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:28.470
that the independent counsel
was a little too independent.

00:05:28.470 --> 00:05:29.610
But obviously there was still a need

00:05:29.610 --> 00:05:31.320
for an independent investigative process,

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:34.410
so in 1999, the DOJ
promulgated regulations

00:05:34.410 --> 00:05:35.370
to establish procedures

00:05:35.370 --> 00:05:37.320
for the appointment of special counsels

00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:38.820
pursuant to the attorney general's

00:05:38.820 --> 00:05:40.920
general administrative hiring authority.

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:42.810
Special counsels serve a similar role

00:05:42.810 --> 00:05:45.480
as independent counsels, but
they have less independence.

00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:47.250
The attorney general
generally has the authority

00:05:47.250 --> 00:05:49.050
to appoint, supervise, and remove them.

00:05:49.050 --> 00:05:49.980
That said, we have an example

00:05:49.980 --> 00:05:52.320
where that kind of independence
was directly challenged.

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:54.420
Richard Nixon's attorney
general, Elliot Richardson,

00:05:54.420 --> 00:05:56.940
hired Archibald Cox as
a special prosecutor

00:05:56.940 --> 00:05:58.590
relating to the Watergate affair.

00:05:58.590 --> 00:06:00.660
The AG had sole authority to fire Cox

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:02.820
and only for extraordinary improprieties,

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:04.860
but Nixon eventually
tired of the investigation

00:06:04.860 --> 00:06:07.050
and ordered Richardson to fire Cox.

00:06:07.050 --> 00:06:09.630
Richardson famously refused
and immediately resigned.

00:06:09.630 --> 00:06:11.790
Nixon then ordered Deputy Attorney General

00:06:11.790 --> 00:06:13.920
William Ruckelshaus to fire Cox.

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:16.110
Ruckelshaus refused and also resigned.

00:06:16.110 --> 00:06:18.810
Nixon finally ordered
Solicitor General Robert Bork,

00:06:18.810 --> 00:06:22.320
the number three at the DOJ,
to fire Cox, and Bork complied.

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:24.420
The special prosecutor was out of a job.

00:06:24.420 --> 00:06:26.550
So while the modern
special counsel regulations

00:06:26.550 --> 00:06:28.740
contemplate independence,
it's hard to refuse

00:06:28.740 --> 00:06:30.810
the head of the executive branch.

00:06:30.810 --> 00:06:32.070
The special counsel is funded

00:06:32.070 --> 00:06:33.780
by the Independent Counsel Appropriation,

00:06:33.780 --> 00:06:36.270
a permanent indefinite
appropriation established

00:06:36.270 --> 00:06:38.820
in the Justice Department's
1988 Appropriations Act,

00:06:38.820 --> 00:06:40.110
and that means that the money comes

00:06:40.110 --> 00:06:42.090
from a permanent Treasury
Department account

00:06:42.090 --> 00:06:44.880
and isn't subject to the
annual appropriations process.

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:46.110
And the attorney general controls

00:06:46.110 --> 00:06:47.310
the special counsel budget,

00:06:47.310 --> 00:06:50.490
so Congress can't defund
Jack Smith in this case.

00:06:50.490 --> 00:06:52.500
The special counsel
reports directly to the AG,

00:06:52.500 --> 00:06:54.060
and if he determines that, say,

00:06:54.060 --> 00:06:55.470
Donald Trump should be charged,

00:06:55.470 --> 00:06:58.470
the special counsel has to
notify Attorney General Garland.

00:06:58.470 --> 00:06:59.647
The regulation says, quote,

00:06:59.647 --> 00:07:01.837
"The special counsel shall
notify the attorney general

00:07:01.837 --> 00:07:04.477
"of events in the course
of his or her investigation

00:07:04.477 --> 00:07:06.577
"in conformity with the
departmental guidelines

00:07:06.577 --> 00:07:08.520
"with respect to urgent reports."

00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:10.020
Congress also has the right to know

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:11.430
what the special counsel is doing.

00:07:11.430 --> 00:07:12.427
The regulation says, quote,

00:07:12.427 --> 00:07:13.717
"The attorney general will notify

00:07:13.717 --> 00:07:15.187
"the chairman ranking minority member

00:07:15.187 --> 00:07:17.767
"of the judiciary committees
of each House of Congress,

00:07:17.767 --> 00:07:19.447
"with an explanation for each action,

00:07:19.447 --> 00:07:21.577
"one, upon appointing a special counsel,

00:07:21.577 --> 00:07:23.797
"two, upon removing any special counsel,

00:07:23.797 --> 00:07:25.267
"and three, upon conclusion

00:07:25.267 --> 00:07:26.737
"of the special counsel's investigation,

00:07:26.737 --> 00:07:29.257
"including to the extent
consistent with applicable law

00:07:29.257 --> 00:07:31.987
"a description and explanation
of instances, if any,

00:07:31.987 --> 00:07:33.487
"in which the attorney general concluded

00:07:33.487 --> 00:07:35.617
"that a proposed action
by a special counsel

00:07:35.617 --> 00:07:37.597
"was so inappropriate or unwarranted

00:07:37.597 --> 00:07:39.577
"under established departmental practices

00:07:39.577 --> 00:07:41.220
"that it should not be pursued."

00:07:41.220 --> 00:07:42.720
So, Congress will be notified

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:45.420
about the outcome of the
Trump-related investigations.

00:07:45.420 --> 00:07:48.660
But another question, why
a special counsel now?

00:07:48.660 --> 00:07:49.590
Although some have questioned

00:07:49.590 --> 00:07:51.330
whether Garland made the right decision,

00:07:51.330 --> 00:07:54.600
the regulations seem to
contemplate a situation like this.

00:07:54.600 --> 00:07:56.400
It states that the attorney
general will appoint

00:07:56.400 --> 00:07:58.710
a special counsel to conduct
certain investigations

00:07:58.710 --> 00:08:01.140
or prosecutions, and those
regulations are set forth

00:08:01.140 --> 00:08:04.950
in 28 C.F.R. 600.1, which
establishes a three-part analysis

00:08:04.950 --> 00:08:07.200
for determining whether to
appoint a special counsel.

00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:09.037
First, the attorney general
must determine that, quote,

00:08:09.037 --> 00:08:10.807
"A criminal investigation of a person

00:08:10.807 --> 00:08:12.007
"or matter is warranted.

00:08:12.007 --> 00:08:13.417
"Then the attorney general must determine

00:08:13.417 --> 00:08:16.087
"whether the investigation
or prosecution of the person

00:08:16.087 --> 00:08:18.187
"or matter by the U.S. Attorney's Office

00:08:18.187 --> 00:08:20.617
"or a Justice Department
litigating division

00:08:20.617 --> 00:08:22.837
"would present either
a conflict of interest

00:08:22.837 --> 00:08:25.477
"for the department or an
extraordinary circumstance.

00:08:25.477 --> 00:08:26.857
"Third, the attorney general must consider

00:08:26.857 --> 00:08:28.357
"whether it would be
in the public interest

00:08:28.357 --> 00:08:30.217
"to appoint an outside special counsel

00:08:30.217 --> 00:08:32.070
"to assume responsibility in the matter."

00:08:32.070 --> 00:08:34.080
So while Garland's order doesn't specify

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:36.140
on what grounds he appointed Smith,

00:08:36.140 --> 00:08:38.460
in his speech announcing the
appointment he said, quote-

00:08:38.460 --> 00:08:40.530
- Based on recent developments,

00:08:40.530 --> 00:08:42.750
including the former
president's announcement

00:08:42.750 --> 00:08:46.050
that he is a candidate for
president in the next election,

00:08:46.050 --> 00:08:48.540
and the sitting president's
stated intention

00:08:48.540 --> 00:08:50.550
to be a candidate as well,

00:08:50.550 --> 00:08:53.910
I have concluded that it
is in the public interest

00:08:53.910 --> 00:08:55.650
to appoint a special counsel.

00:08:55.650 --> 00:08:57.300
- And in the next breath, he points out

00:08:57.300 --> 00:08:59.310
that Donald Trump had
announced his candidacy

00:08:59.310 --> 00:09:00.840
to become president again,

00:09:00.840 --> 00:09:02.310
and President Biden had indicated

00:09:02.310 --> 00:09:03.720
that he would run for reelection.

00:09:03.720 --> 00:09:06.390
So those might indicate either
an extraordinary circumstance

00:09:06.390 --> 00:09:08.820
or a conflict of interest
between the sitting president

00:09:08.820 --> 00:09:10.920
and potential future president.

00:09:10.920 --> 00:09:12.270
Though on the other
hand, there's an argument

00:09:12.270 --> 00:09:13.920
that there's no actual
conflict of interest

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:15.180
that would preclude the DOJ

00:09:15.180 --> 00:09:16.920
from handling investigations normally,

00:09:16.920 --> 00:09:19.440
just like they did before
President Trump announced

00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:20.490
he was running for president.

00:09:20.490 --> 00:09:23.940
He is, after all, just a
U.S. citizen at this point.

00:09:23.940 --> 00:09:25.860
Garland has been overseeing
the investigation

00:09:25.860 --> 00:09:28.530
into the events of
January 6 for two years,

00:09:28.530 --> 00:09:31.170
and any perceived conflict
might have already existed

00:09:31.170 --> 00:09:32.003
during that time,

00:09:32.003 --> 00:09:34.560
and yet there was no special
counsel at that point.

00:09:34.560 --> 00:09:37.140
Though certainly appointing
Smith as special counsel

00:09:37.140 --> 00:09:39.360
has not eliminated political criticism.

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:41.670
Senator Ted Cruz said that
the timing of the appointment

00:09:41.670 --> 00:09:43.770
was proof that the investigation
was politically motivated.

00:09:43.770 --> 00:09:45.847
Cruz said, quote, "Three
days ago, Trump announced

00:09:45.847 --> 00:09:47.437
"and now there is a special counsel.

00:09:47.437 --> 00:09:49.147
"This is Trump derangement syndrome,

00:09:49.147 --> 00:09:50.970
"but this time with a gun and a badge."

00:09:50.970 --> 00:09:52.230
Meanwhile, the House Judiciary Committee

00:09:52.230 --> 00:09:53.550
attacked Smith's impartiality.

00:09:53.550 --> 00:09:55.837
Quote, "Jack Smith and his
buddies have been politicizing

00:09:55.837 --> 00:09:57.097
"Washington for ages,

00:09:57.097 --> 00:09:59.617
"and he's who AG Garland picked
to be the special counsel

00:09:59.617 --> 00:10:01.657
"to, quote, 'investigate' President Trump?

00:10:01.657 --> 00:10:02.490
"Come on."

00:10:02.490 --> 00:10:03.510
And on the other end of the spectrum,

00:10:03.510 --> 00:10:05.370
people who believe that
Trump committed crimes

00:10:05.370 --> 00:10:06.690
and got away with them have scoffed

00:10:06.690 --> 00:10:08.640
at the appointment of a special counsel.

00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:10.440
They contend that this
investigation will end

00:10:10.440 --> 00:10:12.000
the way that the Mueller investigation did

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:13.800
with a report rather than a prosecution,

00:10:13.800 --> 00:10:15.600
though this doesn't seem
to be totally accurate.

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:17.520
First of all, the Mueller
investigation did lead

00:10:17.520 --> 00:10:20.370
to 34 people and three
companies to be indicted.

00:10:20.370 --> 00:10:22.890
And, of course, President Trump
was not one of those people,

00:10:22.890 --> 00:10:24.450
but there are reasons for that.

00:10:24.450 --> 00:10:26.430
You can certainly disagree with them,

00:10:26.430 --> 00:10:28.140
but there were reasons.

00:10:28.140 --> 00:10:29.610
The attorney general has sole authority

00:10:29.610 --> 00:10:31.890
to determine the scope of the
special counsel's inquiry,

00:10:31.890 --> 00:10:33.810
and then President
Trump's attorneys general

00:10:33.810 --> 00:10:35.820
wanted to limit the
special counsel's work.

00:10:35.820 --> 00:10:36.720
And under the mandate

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:38.940
and Mueller's interpretation
of that mandate,

00:10:38.940 --> 00:10:41.550
Mueller was not allowed
to accuse President Trump

00:10:41.550 --> 00:10:42.383
of a crime.

00:10:42.383 --> 00:10:43.710
Now, Mueller was also constrained

00:10:43.710 --> 00:10:45.210
by the Office of Legal Counsel memo

00:10:45.210 --> 00:10:47.010
that says a sitting
president cannot be indicted

00:10:47.010 --> 00:10:48.240
while he's in office.

00:10:48.240 --> 00:10:50.130
That memo's conclusion is controversial

00:10:50.130 --> 00:10:51.960
and the Supreme Court
has never ruled on that,

00:10:51.960 --> 00:10:53.850
however, the Justice Department employees

00:10:53.850 --> 00:10:56.250
do follow the OLC memo.

00:10:56.250 --> 00:10:58.590
So the Mueller report
outlined over 10 instances

00:10:58.590 --> 00:11:00.720
of obstruction of justice
that implicated Trump,

00:11:00.720 --> 00:11:03.660
however, even if Mueller had
the authority to charge Trump,

00:11:03.660 --> 00:11:06.030
he believed that he was
bound by the OLC guidance

00:11:06.030 --> 00:11:07.890
on indicting sitting presidents.

00:11:07.890 --> 00:11:09.990
The Smith appointment as special counsel

00:11:09.990 --> 00:11:10.920
is markedly different

00:11:10.920 --> 00:11:13.260
because Garland gave him a
broad mandate to investigate

00:11:13.260 --> 00:11:15.630
and prosecute Trump if
the evidence is there.

00:11:15.630 --> 00:11:17.220
The posture of the case
is also very different.

00:11:17.220 --> 00:11:19.320
There were multiple grand
juries weighing evidence

00:11:19.320 --> 00:11:20.970
that Trump interfered
with the certification

00:11:20.970 --> 00:11:23.040
of the 2020 election and
that he broke the law

00:11:23.040 --> 00:11:25.140
by taking documents to Mar-a-Lago.

00:11:25.140 --> 00:11:27.750
In addition, there's no
memorandum or court case anywhere

00:11:27.750 --> 00:11:30.300
that suggests it's illegal
to prosecute an ex-president.

00:11:30.300 --> 00:11:32.100
And Smith will have to
follow the DOJ's guidance

00:11:32.100 --> 00:11:34.560
on when it is appropriate to
recommend criminal charges.

00:11:34.560 --> 00:11:39.300
Section 9-27.220 of the DOJ's
manual outlines the grounds

00:11:39.300 --> 00:11:41.640
for commencing or declining prosecution.

00:11:41.640 --> 00:11:43.597
It states, quote, "The
attorney for the government

00:11:43.597 --> 00:11:45.937
"should commence or
recommend federal prosecution

00:11:45.937 --> 00:11:48.607
"if he/she believes that
the person's conduct

00:11:48.607 --> 00:11:49.897
"constitutes a federal offense

00:11:49.897 --> 00:11:51.037
"and that the admissible evidence

00:11:51.037 --> 00:11:52.237
"will probably be sufficient

00:11:52.237 --> 00:11:54.037
"to obtain and sustain a conviction

00:11:54.037 --> 00:11:55.957
"unless, one, the prosecution would serve

00:11:55.957 --> 00:11:57.787
"no substantial federal interest;

00:11:57.787 --> 00:12:00.637
"two, the person is subject
to effective prosecution

00:12:00.637 --> 00:12:02.347
"in another jurisdiction;

00:12:02.347 --> 00:12:04.327
"or three, there exists an adequate

00:12:04.327 --> 00:12:06.600
"non-criminal alternative to prosecution."

00:12:06.600 --> 00:12:08.520
So now you might be
wondering who is Jack Smith,

00:12:08.520 --> 00:12:09.990
the new special counsel?

00:12:09.990 --> 00:12:12.630
Well, he is a 50-year-old
graduate of Harvard Law School

00:12:12.630 --> 00:12:13.980
who started his career as a prosecutor

00:12:13.980 --> 00:12:15.990
in the Manhattan District
Attorney's Office.

00:12:15.990 --> 00:12:18.480
Smith then moved to the U.S.
Attorney's Office in Brooklyn

00:12:18.480 --> 00:12:20.610
where he worked on
public corruption cases.

00:12:20.610 --> 00:12:21.930
Smith then spent two years

00:12:21.930 --> 00:12:23.340
as the investigation coordinator

00:12:23.340 --> 00:12:24.870
in the office of the prosecutor

00:12:24.870 --> 00:12:26.760
at the International
Criminal Court in the Hague.

00:12:26.760 --> 00:12:28.800
Smith supervised high
profile investigations,

00:12:28.800 --> 00:12:30.690
so people wanted for
war crimes and genocide.

00:12:30.690 --> 00:12:32.970
From 2010 to 2015, Smith was chief

00:12:32.970 --> 00:12:34.830
of the DOJ's Public Integrity Section

00:12:34.830 --> 00:12:36.870
which investigates public
corruption allegations.

00:12:36.870 --> 00:12:37.800
And while in that role,

00:12:37.800 --> 00:12:39.870
Smith investigated high
profile politicians

00:12:39.870 --> 00:12:40.950
on both sides of the aisle,

00:12:40.950 --> 00:12:43.830
which really seemed to piss
off just about everyone.

00:12:43.830 --> 00:12:45.810
The Public Integrity
Section was under fire

00:12:45.810 --> 00:12:47.970
for a case against former
senator Ted Stevens

00:12:47.970 --> 00:12:48.803
which collapsed.

00:12:48.803 --> 00:12:50.310
During Smith's first
few months on the job,

00:12:50.310 --> 00:12:51.420
he closed investigations

00:12:51.420 --> 00:12:53.460
into several Republican
members of Congress.

00:12:53.460 --> 00:12:54.690
During his years as chief,

00:12:54.690 --> 00:12:56.250
the Public Integrity Section garnered

00:12:56.250 --> 00:12:57.270
a successful conviction

00:12:57.270 --> 00:12:59.670
against former Republican
governor Bob McDonnell,

00:12:59.670 --> 00:13:02.580
which was later unanimously
overturned by the Supreme Court.

00:13:02.580 --> 00:13:04.260
And then the Obama administration

00:13:04.260 --> 00:13:07.050
chose not to continue
prosecution after that reversal.

00:13:07.050 --> 00:13:08.490
Smith also pursued a case

00:13:08.490 --> 00:13:10.380
against Democratic vice
presidential candidate

00:13:10.380 --> 00:13:12.210
and former senator John Edwards,

00:13:12.210 --> 00:13:14.730
which resulted in a hung
jury and a mistrial.

00:13:14.730 --> 00:13:16.290
Smith also helped lead the investigation

00:13:16.290 --> 00:13:18.930
into Democrat Bob Menendez,
though he left the department

00:13:18.930 --> 00:13:20.610
a few months before the indictment,

00:13:20.610 --> 00:13:22.260
though Menendez was later acquitted.

00:13:22.260 --> 00:13:24.720
His team won a conviction against
Representative Rick Renzi,

00:13:24.720 --> 00:13:26.190
a Republican from Arizona

00:13:26.190 --> 00:13:27.840
who was sentenced to
three years in prison,

00:13:27.840 --> 00:13:30.270
though Trump pardoned Renzi
right before he left office

00:13:30.270 --> 00:13:31.470
in early 2021.

00:13:31.470 --> 00:13:32.303
For his own part,

00:13:32.303 --> 00:13:33.960
Smith said that the pace
of the Trump investigations

00:13:33.960 --> 00:13:35.880
would not slow down under his watch.

00:13:35.880 --> 00:13:37.507
He said, quote, "I intend to conduct

00:13:37.507 --> 00:13:38.707
"the assigned investigations,

00:13:38.707 --> 00:13:40.747
"and any prosecutions
that may result from them,

00:13:40.747 --> 00:13:42.427
"independently and in the best traditions

00:13:42.427 --> 00:13:43.830
"of the Department of Justice."

00:13:43.830 --> 00:13:45.840
Mr. Smith said that in
a statement he vowed

00:13:45.840 --> 00:13:47.910
that the investigations would
move forward expeditiously,

00:13:47.910 --> 00:13:50.790
quote, "To whatever outcome
the facts and law dictate."

00:13:50.790 --> 00:13:52.387
Though the "New York Times"
reports that Smith, quote,

00:13:52.387 --> 00:13:54.187
"Will remain in the
Netherlands for some time,

00:13:54.187 --> 00:13:55.447
"according to the Justice Department,

00:13:55.447 --> 00:13:58.530
"in order to recover from
a recent bicycle accident."

00:13:58.530 --> 00:14:00.030
Though the 11th Circuit
held oral arguments

00:14:00.030 --> 00:14:02.220
in the Mar-a-Lago case on the
Tuesday before Thanksgiving,

00:14:02.220 --> 00:14:04.140
and although Smith remains overseas,

00:14:04.140 --> 00:14:06.060
the DOJ filed notice
that he was on the case.

00:14:06.060 --> 00:14:08.677
Quote, "Special Counsel Smith
has reviewed the filings

00:14:08.677 --> 00:14:10.567
"in this litigation and
approves all of the arguments

00:14:10.567 --> 00:14:12.157
"that have been presented in the briefs

00:14:12.157 --> 00:14:14.977
"and will be discussed at the
oral argument in this case.

00:14:14.977 --> 00:14:16.747
"Special Counsel Smith's
name will be affixed

00:14:16.747 --> 00:14:19.147
"to future pleadings filed in all matters

00:14:19.147 --> 00:14:20.880
"falling within his appointment."

00:14:20.880 --> 00:14:22.980
So while the appointment of
Jack Smith as special counsel

00:14:22.980 --> 00:14:25.470
gives a little bit for everyone to hate,

00:14:25.470 --> 00:14:27.540
net-net it's probably not good news

00:14:27.540 --> 00:14:29.280
for former President Trump.

00:14:29.280 --> 00:14:32.310
Certainly it probably means
delays in the short term,

00:14:32.310 --> 00:14:34.830
but Jack Smith has an
advantage over Robert Mueller

00:14:34.830 --> 00:14:36.630
who had to hire
investigators and prosecutors

00:14:36.630 --> 00:14:37.860
and start from scratch.

00:14:37.860 --> 00:14:39.420
Smith gets to hit the ground running.

00:14:39.420 --> 00:14:42.810
He'll supervise DOJ prosecutors
who are already on the case,

00:14:42.810 --> 00:14:44.850
and the DOJ has issued subpoenas,

00:14:44.850 --> 00:14:47.010
served a search warrant on Mar-a-Lago,

00:14:47.010 --> 00:14:48.450
and are in court battling Trump

00:14:48.450 --> 00:14:50.490
over the appointment
of the special master.

00:14:50.490 --> 00:14:52.470
And the special counsel
has hit the ground running.

00:14:52.470 --> 00:14:54.540
Smith now oversees the
ongoing investigation

00:14:54.540 --> 00:14:56.790
into Trump's efforts to
overturn the 2020 election,

00:14:56.790 --> 00:14:59.010
and Smith's office just issued subpoenas

00:14:59.010 --> 00:15:01.500
to elections officials in
Allegheny County, Pennsylvania,

00:15:01.500 --> 00:15:03.840
Dane County, Wisconsin,
Wayne County, Michigan,

00:15:03.840 --> 00:15:05.310
and Maricopa County, Arizona.

00:15:05.310 --> 00:15:06.990
The subpoenas seek
documents and communications

00:15:06.990 --> 00:15:08.280
they had with 19 people,

00:15:08.280 --> 00:15:10.920
including Trump and some of
his most well-known associates

00:15:10.920 --> 00:15:13.830
like looney lawyer Lin Wood,
Sidney the Kraken Powell,

00:15:13.830 --> 00:15:16.440
Rudy Giuliani, Cleta
Mitchell, and Bernie Kerik.

00:15:16.440 --> 00:15:18.060
Realistically, the appointment of Smith

00:15:18.060 --> 00:15:21.000
probably does nothing to mollify
the political critics of it

00:15:21.000 --> 00:15:23.910
who will say that this is
a political prosecution,

00:15:23.910 --> 00:15:26.250
but still, appointing a special counsel

00:15:26.250 --> 00:15:29.100
is probably still better than main justice

00:15:29.100 --> 00:15:32.700
under the aegis of AG Garland
doing the prosecution.

00:15:32.700 --> 00:15:34.290
And while trying to figure out
what's going to happen next

00:15:34.290 --> 00:15:35.700
is reading tea leaves,

00:15:35.700 --> 00:15:38.010
it probably doesn't mean that
nothing is going to happen.

00:15:38.010 --> 00:15:39.990
In other words, Garland probably would not

00:15:39.990 --> 00:15:41.490
have appointed a special counsel

00:15:41.490 --> 00:15:44.010
if he had determined that no
prosecution was warranted.

00:15:44.010 --> 00:15:45.270
And I think that Smith's experience shows

00:15:45.270 --> 00:15:47.370
that he prosecutes hard cases.

00:15:47.370 --> 00:15:49.380
Often the DOJ will only take a case

00:15:49.380 --> 00:15:51.450
if they think that they are sure to win,

00:15:51.450 --> 00:15:54.390
but it looks like Smith's record
doesn't follow that adage.

00:15:54.390 --> 00:15:57.103
Public integrity cases
are notoriously difficult,

00:15:57.103 --> 00:15:59.370
and the reason that the
Supreme Court has overturned

00:15:59.370 --> 00:16:01.290
some of these convictions
is that drawing the line

00:16:01.290 --> 00:16:04.530
between political action and
bribery is very tough to do,

00:16:04.530 --> 00:16:05.760
let alone to get convictions

00:16:05.760 --> 00:16:07.500
in these politically sensitive cases.

00:16:07.500 --> 00:16:09.570
Though here, at least
with respect to documents,

00:16:09.570 --> 00:16:12.660
that is a much, much simpler
case than political bribery.

00:16:12.660 --> 00:16:14.190
But while Special Counsel Smith is resting

00:16:14.190 --> 00:16:16.230
his bicycle injury and
getting up to speed,

00:16:16.230 --> 00:16:18.450
he'll want some legal
audiobooks to listen to.

00:16:18.450 --> 00:16:20.257
He might enjoy John
Grisham's new legal thriller

00:16:20.257 --> 00:16:22.590
"The Judge's List" on
